      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost by Material Type"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Utah's harsh freeze-thaw cycles, high-elevation UV rays, and heavy winter snow lines make material selection highly important."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles ($4.50 \u2013 $8.25 / sq. ft.): The most popular and cost-effective option. They handle standard high winds well and look great, typically lasting 20 to 30 years. Expect to pay $9,000 \u2013 $16,500 total."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Standing Seam Metal Roofing ($12.00 \u2013 $30.00 / sq. ft.): Highly efficient at shedding heavy snow loads. While they require a higher upfront investment of $18,000 \u2013 $32,000+, they easily last 50+ years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Composite / Synthetic Shingles ($8.00 \u2013 $15.00 / sq. ft.): Built to mimic luxury wood shakes or slate, these premium materials offer maximum impact resistance against Utah hailstorms. Total costs range from $16,000 \u2013 $28,000."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Local Cost Factors to Keep in Mind"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Location Matters: Labor rates in Salt Lake County tend to run slightly higher than quotes found in Northern Utah or Cache County."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The \"Tear-Off\" Factor: If your historic or older home has multiple layers of old shingles, codes require them to be entirely stripped away. This can add $1,500 to $4,500 in labor and dumpster disposal fees."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hidden Damage: If a contractor uncovers rotted plywood decking beneath your old roof, expect to pay $70 to $120 per sheet to replace it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To get the most accurate estimate, it is best to consult local professionals like IWC Roofing Utah or Vertex Roofing who understand local building codes and specific climate requirements.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "IWC Roofing Utah",
            "link": "https://iwcroofing.com/utah/pricing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow this down, could you tell me your approximate location in Utah, the estimated square footage of your home, and if you are dealing with any active storm damage or leaks?"
      },
